  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 345 PM MDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nThe long term will be starting off quite interesting on the back\nside of of the cutoff low that will bring widespread\nprecipitation to the region and cooler air as well. Thursday\nmorning we'll see widespread precipitation still ongoing, with\nmostly rain for our lower elevation zones, rain/snow to pure \nsnow for our higher elevations, and likely snow in the \nmountains. Of note though will be morning temperatures and their\neffect on our mid to lower elevation locations. While we're not\nexpecting the first widespread winter storm of the season quite\nthis early, some ensembles and deterministic guidance are \nstarting to hint that we may get temperatures cold enough \nspilling just off of the high terrain that we could see some \nnear surface wintry mix, possibly off the Laramie Summit and \neven coming down into the Cheyenne area. Even if this does occur\nthough, being the first cold spell of the year combined with \nwarm ground temperatures, accumulations are not likely or will \nmelt very quickly, but the chance is there. Nonetheless, morning\nsnow will likely be ongoing at the Laramie Summit and for both \nthe Sierra-Madres and Snowies, but QPF will be quickly dwindling\nand accumulations will be lessening by the early afternoon. \nOtherwise expect a breezy day, particularly east of the I-25 \ncorridor this time, with temperatures struggling to make it out \nof the 40's to mid 50's thanks to the lingering cold air and \ncloud cover.\n\nThat should begin to change into Friday morning as clouds will\nbegin moving out of the region and high pressure ridging starts\nto overtake as we move into the weekend. At first though this\nwill have a cooling effect overnight, and the current forecast\ncalls for all zones to reach freezing or below. Many zones\ncould see hard freeze conditions as well, and it's becoming \nmore and more likely that freeze products will be necessary. The\nNBM indicates a 30-100% probability of morning lows below \nfreezing, with the \"warmest\" locations and lowest probabilities \naround Wheatland and the northern Nebraska Panhandle. \nTemperatures begin to rebound into the afternoon, with highs in \nthe 50's to near 60 for the day. Following this we'll continue \nto see a warming trend with temperatures back around 10 degrees \nabove normal as the 60's and 70's return under stagnant high \npressure ridging. A cutoff upper level low along the Baja \nPeninsula in Mexico should help to stave off even stronger \nridging from building in, but it will continue to promote the \nreturn of above normal temperatures nonetheless. Pressure \ngradients may strengthen enough on Saturday to bring a high wind\nevent for our western zones, with in house guidance already \nstarting to spark a 40% probability for the Bordeaux area, and \naround a 20-30% probability for our other wind prone locations. \nThis may be the most noteworthy forecast element as we move into\nnext weekend, with an otherwise quiet and pleasant forecast on \ntap.",
